#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# kelvin_kei

#% text
# `kelvin_kei(nu, x)` gives the Kelvin function `kei_nu(x)`, the imaginary part of `exp(-nu*pi*im/2)*bessel_k(nu, x*exp(pi*im/4))`.

#% text
# `kelvin_kei(x)` gives `kei_0(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The definition is DLMF 10.61.2, for `x > 0`; `kei_0(0)` is `-pi/4`.

#% item
# Numerical values need `bessel_y` at complex arguments, which is not evaluated yet: machine arguments give an unevaluated expression.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Symbolic arguments stay as they are:

#% cell
kelvin_kei(x)
#% output
#= kelvin_kei(x)

#% cell
kelvin_kei(nu, x)
#% output
#= kelvin_kei(nu, x)

#% cell
kelvin_kei(2, x)
#% output
#= kelvin_kei(2, x)

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A machine argument is not reduced to a number:

#% cell
kelvin_kei(1.0)
#% output
#= -0.4949946365187199


#% text
# Machine values of any order:

#% cell
[kelvin_kei(2.5), kelvin_kei(1, 8.)]
#% output
#= [-0.11069609915567469, -0.0015669747999993433]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `kelvin_ker`, `kelvin_ber`, `kelvin_bei`, `bessel_k`
